    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and both tests pass along with a clean build (which also regenerates DTOs from the OpenAPI spec). \n\nThe change is complete. Here's a summary of what I implemented for soft-delete of owners:\n\n**Domain model** (`Owner.java`)\n- Added a `deleted` boolean field (mapped to a new `deleted` column) with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`. A newly constructed owner defaults to `false`.\n\n**Deletion behavior** (`ClinicServiceImpl.deleteOwner`)\n- Changed from a hard `ownerRepository.delete(owner)` to a soft-delete: it flags `deleted = true` and persists via `save`, retaining the row. The controller's `DELETE /api/owners/{id}` already returns `204`, and `GET` still finds the retained row \u2014 now reporting `deleted: true`.\n\n**Duplicate & identity checks ignore deleted owners**\n- `Households.findMembers` (backing the blocking `HouseholdDuplicateValidator`) now filters out `deleted` owners, so a soft-deleted owner no longer counts as a household member \u2014 a normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only match is deleted.\n- `PossibleDuplicateDetector.findMatch` (the soft identity check) likewise skips deleted owners.\n- I scoped the filter to just these two duplicate/identity components rather than the shared `findByLastNameIgnoreCase` query, so unrelated logic like namesake counting is unaffected.\n\n**API contract** (`openapi.yml`)\n- Added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema (and to its `required` list), which generates the `deleted` field on `OwnerDto`; MapStruct maps it automatically from the entity.\n\n**Persistence schemas**\n- Added a `deleted BOOLEAN` column (default `FALSE`, not null) to all four schema files (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql), using the `DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` ordering HSQLDB/H2 require.\n\nI also added `@Mapping(target = \"deleted\", ignore = true)` to the create mapper (`toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)`) to match the existing explicit-ignore convention for server-derived fields.",
